All fixes are implemented and verified. Here's a summary.

What was changed

1. servo_serial_bridge.py (both copies: pca9685_multiple and mearm_bringup)

Problem: Every incoming /arm_joint_commands message was written to the STM32 immediately, with a blocking time.sleep(0.02) per joint (80 ms blocked per callback). Commands can arrive at up to 100 Hz (ros2_control write cycle), flooding the serial port and making the physical servos jerk.

Fix:

Rate-limited writes per joint (new max_write_hz param, default 20 Hz) — a joint is only re-sent if enough time has elapsed OR the value changed meaningfully (min_change_rad, default 0.01 rad).
Removed the blocking per-joint sleep; serial is now non-blocking (timeout=0) so a slow STM32 can't stall the ROS callback thread.
Added a lock and error handling so a transient serial error doesn't kill the node.
2. mearm_visual_servoing_cpp_node.cpp + params YAML

Problem: max_joint_speed_rad_s defaulted to 1.0 rad/s and each trajectory used time_from_start = 0.2 s, so RViz snapped instantly to each new target and the arm moved too fast.

Fix:

max_joint_speed_rad_s: 1.0 → 0.5 rad/s (hard cap on per-tick joint step)
smoothing_alpha: 0.25 → 0.15 (slower EMA smoothing)
time_from_start: 0.2 s → 0.5 s (gentler trajectory duration)
These apply to both the C++ node's built-in defaults and the YAML config.

Important: don't run two serial bridges

Your bringup launch (mearm_bringup bringup.launch.py use_mock_hardware:=false) already spawns servo_serial_bridge and writes to /dev/ttyACM0. Your separate command:


ros2 run pca9685_multiple servo_serial_bridge --ros-args -p port:=/dev/ttyACM0
starts a second writer to the same port. Two nodes writing to one STM32 serial port will corrupt the command stream and cause jerky/erratic servo movement.

Use only one of these:

Option A (recommended): let the launch file spawn the bridge — drop the manual ros2 run ... servo_serial_bridge line.
Option B: run the bridge manually and disable it in the launch by keeping use_mock_hardware:=true (but then real hardware won't move).
